# ===========================================================================================
#  What does not belong in the repository
# ===========================================================================================
#
#  Two rules decide everything here. Anything a toolchain can regenerate from source is not
#  committed, and anything that is a measurement is. That is why the .log files from synthesis are
#  excluded but the numbers extracted from them live in docs/ as prose and tables: a log is an
#  artifact, a measurement is a result.

# --- the toolchain itself. 16,218 files and hundreds of megabytes. -------------------------
# Install an OSS CAD Suite here and point OSS_CAD at it; see the README.
tools/

# The bench harness lives in the ROOT tools directory, and it is source, not toolchain. bench_run.py is
# what enforces the rule that nothing is flashed until the exact image about to be flashed has been
# archived; listen.py is what captures what the board said. Losing either loses the ability to reproduce
# a measurement, so both are committed.
#
# Four lines rather than one, because of how git reads these. A pattern cannot re-include a file whose
# parent directory is excluded, so the root directory has to be un-excluded before anything inside it can
# be; and every pattern here is anchored with a leading slash so that the nested tools directories under
# firmware/ keep the behaviour they have always had, which is to be ignored.
!/tools/
/tools/*
!/tools/*.py

# --- compiled output ------------------------------------------------------------------------
build/
*.exe
*.hex
# THE ARCHIVE IS THE EXCEPTION, AND IT IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THE ARCHIVE.
#
# A .hex is normally a build product and regenerable. The ones under bench-archive/ are not: each is the
# exact image that produced the serial log sitting beside it, and the rule on this bench is that any
# state the board has ever been in can be returned to by flashing one file. A backup that exists only on
# one disk is one disk away from not existing, so these are committed. The .elf, .lst, .sym and .eep
# that arduino-cli writes alongside are excluded in bench-archive/.gitignore -- they are 95% of the
# bytes and none of them is needed to put the board back.
!bench-archive/**/*.hex
*.elf
*.bin
*.o
*.obj
*.a

# Cross-compiled test binaries for the ARM boards. The naming convention is deliberate: a file
# ending _arm is a build product of the matching .c, never a source file.
*_arm

# Icarus Verilog compiled simulations
*.vvp

# --- tool logs. The conclusions drawn from these are written up in docs/ instead. -----------
firmware/bench-one/fpga/rtl/y*.log
firmware/bench-one/fpga/**/synth.log
firmware/bench-one/fpga/**/s.log

# --- rendered frames, which are regenerated by the render tests ------------------------------
*.ppm

# --- agent skill scratch output, regenerated on every run -----------------------------------
.claude/skills/*/scratch/

# --- Python and editor litter ---------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*~
*.swp

# The two shipped FPGA bitstreams ARE tracked, despite the *.bin rule above. They are build products,
# but they are the build products you need on the bench on the day the board arrives, and they cost
# 270 kB. build.sh regenerates them identically; this is insurance against the toolchain, not a
# substitute for it.
!firmware/bench-one/fpga/ddr3_ice40/bitstreams/*.bin

# Host test build products. run_host_tests.sh regenerates them.
.hosttest/

# The car's deploy images ARE tracked, despite *.hex / *.bin above: they are exactly what flash.sh and
# flash_windows.ps1 put on the boards, pinned by deploy/firmware/SHA256SUMS, and run_all_tests.sh
# proves each one rebuilds byte-identical from source. The bundle is regenerated by make_bundle.sh.
!deploy/firmware/*.hex
!deploy/firmware/*.bin
deploy/car-bundle.tar.gz
firmware/tests/host/out/

# matrix-engine testbench vectors, regenerated by accel/tb/run_tb.sh
accel/tb/*.hex
accel/tb/counts.vh
accel/tb/*.vvp

# accelerator build outputs (rebuilt by each component build script); the Pi bundle is made by accel/make_pi_bundle.sh
accel/zynq/out/
accel/pi/out/
accel/gpu/teensy/out/
accel/accel-pi-bundle.tar.gz
accel/.*.tmp.sh
accel/gpu/out/
accel/gpu/fpga/probe/*
!accel/gpu/fpga/probe/ps7_bd_wrapper_ports.txt
!accel/gpu/fpga/probe/ps7_bd_addr.txt
# DMA co-simulation: generated IP, project, vectors and logs
accel/cosim/prj/
accel/cosim/ip/
accel/cosim/*.hex
accel/cosim/counts.vh
accel/cosim/*.txt
.Xil/
accel/cosim/.ip_user_files/
accel/cosim/*.log
accel/llm/out/
# GPU render-core scene runs: work directories written by sim/run_core.sh
accel/gpu/sim/py_scenes/
